{
  "id": "summary-ranges",
  "questionId": "summary-ranges",
  "slug": "summary-ranges",
  "title": "Summary Ranges",
  "difficulty": "Easy",
  "category": "Merge intervals",
  "patterns": [
    "merge intervals",
    "arrays"
  ],
  "lists": [
    "interview-150"
  ],
  "hints": [
    "since the array is sorted and has unique values, two numbers are 'consecutive' exactly when nums[i+1] == nums[i] + 1",
    "walk through the array keeping track of where the current run started",
    "whenever the next number breaks the run (isn't exactly one more than the current number), close off the range [runStart, currentNumber] and start a new run at the next number",
    "don't forget to close off and record the final run after the loop ends"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "forgetting to flush the last run after the loop finishes",
    "comparing nums[i+1] - nums[i-1] or some other off-by-one instead of nums[i+1] - nums[i] == 1",
    "not handling the empty array case, which should return an empty list rather than crashing or returning [[]]"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a sorted array of unique integers `nums`.\n\n**Return the smallest sorted list of ranges that exactly covers every number in `nums`, where each range is represented as [start, end].**\n\nIf a range covers only a single number, still represent it as [start, start].\n",
    "constraints": [
      "0 <= nums.length <= 25",
      "-2^31 <= nums[i] <= 2^31 - 1",
      "all values in nums are unique",
      "nums is sorted in strictly increasing order"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "empty array (return an empty list of ranges)",
      "single element array",
      "every number is part of one single consecutive run",
      "no two numbers are consecutive (every range is a single point)",
      "negative numbers"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [0,1,2,4,5,7]",
        "output": "[[0,2],[4,5],[7,7]]",
        "expectedOutput": "[[0,2],[4,5],[7,7]]",
        "explanation": "0,1,2 are consecutive; 4,5 are consecutive; 7 stands alone"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "nums = [0,2,3,4,6,8,9]",
